title: Overview - Phase Timeline (v1.0)

A Gantt-style view of the parse pipeline: the four TW parser phases run left-to-right across the top, and ten tracks show ''what happens to each piece of state'' along the way. Sibling to [[Overview - Parse Pipeline (v1.0)]] — same engine, time as the x-axis instead of as numbered steps.

[img[cm-v1-phase-timeline.svg]]

!! How to read the chart

* The top bar shows the four parser phases (A) `instantiateRules`, (B) `parsePragmas` (wrapped), (C) `parseBlocks` / `parseInlineRun`, (D) Render. Phase widths are illustrative, not real-time.
* Each row is a ''track'' — one piece of state or one activity. A solid bar means the track is actively being mutated; a dashed bar means it's in effect but read-only.
* Diamond markers (◇) are ''instantaneous events'' — flag flips, single function calls.

!! What the tracks show

; Row 1 — Rule init()
: Every TW wikirule's `init()` runs once during `instantiateRules`. For Custom Markup, this is where `cmblock`, `cminline`, `markdown-table`, `markdown-newline`, and `front-matter` each get a chance to set up.

; Row 2 — cmRegistry
: `cmblock.init()` constructs the registry, runs `loadAllMarkers` + `loadGlobalPragmas`, parses the type field, and schedules the amendment. After that the registry is alive (but mostly read) until parse ends.

; Row 3 — registry.active
: The set of `open` literals enabled for this parser. Grows in two waves: type-field vocabs in phase A, then more vocabs from `\importcustom` in phase B. After phase B the set is frozen and every match consults it.

; Row 4 — registry.disabledCoreRules
: Union of `disable-core-rules` from every activated vocab. Populated in A, applied in B (see row 5).

; Row 5 — TW core wikirules
: At the start of phase B the wrapped `parsePragmas` calls `parser.amendRules("except", disabledCoreRules)` (◇ red diamond). From then on the reduced set is in effect — e.g. Fountain has removed `heading list`, Markdown has removed `strikethrough codeinline list underscore table`.

; Row 6 — parser flags
: Two booleans: `_cmAmendScheduled` flips in phase A (so the wrap is set up once), `_cmPragmasDone` flips at the end of phase B (so the active-set filter kicks in for the body parse, and `matchIndex` is refreshed for the four affected rules).

; Row 7 — User pragmas
: `\custom`, `\importcustom`, `\debugmarker` parse in the middle of phase B — after the core-rule amendment, before `_cmPragmasDone` flips. `\importcustom` writes back to row 3.

; Row 8 — findNextMatch
: Behaves differently across phases. Before `_cmPragmasDone`, accepts any marker match so the rule survives TW's pruning; after, the active-set filter skips inactive marker positions instead of consuming them.

; Row 9 — parse()
: In phase C, body consumption per kind, element emission with `wltc` + `wltc-l<N>` + `marker.classes`, and attribute scrubbing (sandbox iframe; deny object/embed/frame/frameset; strip `javascript:` URLs).

; Row 10 — Render
: TW turns the parse tree into a widget tree into DOM. If the marker has a `debug` field (or a `\debugmarker` override), a codeblock is emitted alongside the normal render.

!! The four "why"s

See the footer in the diagram for the full list. In short:

# `init()` can't call `amendRules` (rule arrays not yet assigned), so it's deferred via a one-shot wrap of `parser.parsePragmas`.
# The master regex must cover ''every'' marker, or TW prunes the rule before pragmas activate new vocabs.
# `_cmPragmasDone` gates the active-set filter so the rule survives pruning AND filters cleanly post-pragmas.
# `matchIndex` must be refreshed after the flag flip because TW caches the pre-pragma find result.

